# PGlite independent-session cases

Status: accepted scope ruling, 2026-11-02, DECKO-818 cutover lead.

The installed PGlite 0.5.10 matrix backend opens one client and explicitly
reports `secondHandle: "same-session"` in `tests/card/actions/backend-matrix.ts`.
Its observer uses that client and refuses reads inside an open write
transaction. This backend cannot supply the independent sessions required by
the original matrix's concurrent-writes and concurrent-reads cases.
The [PGlite documentation](https://pglite.dev/docs/) independently describes
its single exclusive database connection; sharing access does not create
independent PostgreSQL backend sessions.

Those two frozen occurrences receive the named disposition
**capability-inapplicable**. They remain visible as two skipped cases, never
as passes or retirement. Every other frozen occurrence retains the original
three-category disposition rule. The five-case matrix test file and its
pinned source hash remain unchanged.

Before each approved skip, the shared skip function asserts that the opened
backend still reports `same-session`. Missing evidence, `none`, or an
independent capability cannot retain this skip classification. A backend
reporting independent handles follows the original concurrent assertions.
Controls exercise both approved cases and reject changed capability reports.

Native MySQL's original independent-session concurrent-writes and
concurrent-reads cases passed in the frozen 42a full-run report. The
qualification contract names that raw report and source identity.
Native PostgreSQL 16.15 has separate supplemental concurrency evidence in
[the DECKO-256 report](../runs/engine-checks-decko256-2026-11-02/REPORT.md).
Five original assertion bodies, including concurrent writes and isolated
concurrent reads, passed on two independent pg.Client connections in an owned
VM scratch container using the existing postgres:16-alpine image. The original
assertion module hash is
`d0809911562b7f80b3389c7fc9dc17e6b4e2380238e3a6f4fdaf3af8011bfbaa`;
the final bundle hash is
`3dd9c2ba47a439bc75766569fed9dfe37b797822ce278c50c728193b393bbe39`.
This execution uses a Vitest-to-Node assertion adapter with 18 controls;
it is not an unmodified Vitest run or complete runtime qualification. The
earlier failed tunnel report is retained and is not accepted evidence.
These PGlite dispositions grant no complete-runtime or release acceptance.
